Short example (simple or sparsely evidenced repository):
acme/config-check is a CLI that validates YAML service configuration before deployment. It checks required values, reports failures with file and field names, and exits nonzero on invalid input, making it suitable for manual checks and CI jobs that gate deployment configuration changes.

Medium example (several evidenced capabilities):
acme/orders handles online purchases from checkout through order acceptance. Its HTTP API validates a customer's basket, checks current inventory, and requests payment authorization before recording the order. It publishes order-created and order-cancelled events for the fulfillment workflow, and exposes status endpoints used by the storefront to show progress.

The repository also contains a worker that reconciles payment callbacks with stored orders and retries publication of pending events. These capabilities support storefront checkout, asynchronous payment completion, and recovery when an external service is temporarily unavailable.

Long example (complex repository with detailed evidence):
acme/catalog provides product discovery and catalog management for storefront applications and merchandising workflows. It combines descriptive product data with regional pricing, inventory availability, and publication status so clients can search for items and decide which offers to display. Public API routes support keyword search, category browsing, and retrieval of individual products, while an administrative API updates product attributes and controls whether entries are visible in a particular market.

The TypeScript web application is a catalog-management interface. It lets merchandising users review imported product records, edit descriptions and classifications, preview regional offers, and submit publication changes. A separate import worker accepts supplier feeds, validates the required product identifiers and fields, and records rejected entries so corrected feeds can be processed later. The repository also supports bulk updates for campaigns that affect several products at once.

Background consumers apply inventory and pricing updates from upstream systems to the read model used by product searches. Changes to published entries produce catalog events for downstream storefront caches. These workflows make the repository relevant to product search, supplier-feed ingestion, merchandising, regional offer management, and keeping storefront product information synchronized.

